// Title: New TimeProviders Registered With Uncommon DLL Name
// Sigma ID: e88a6ddc-74f7-463b-9b26-f69fc0d2ce85
// Status: test | Level: high
// Author: frack113
// Date: 2022-06-19 | Modified: 2024-03-26
// ATT&CK: persistence, privilege-escalation, T1547.003
// Description: Detects processes setting a new DLL in DllName in under
//   HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\W32Time\TimeProvider. Adversaries may abuse
//   time providers to execute DLLs when the system boots. The Windows Time service (W32Time) enables
//   time synchronization across and within domains.
// False positives:
//   - Unknown
// References:
//   - https://github.com/redcanaryco/atomic-red-team/blob/f339e7da7d05f6057fdfcdd3742bfcf365fee2a9/atomics/T1547.003/T1547.003.md
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/05d1031f537e830b76da53bae2400c5814c89165/rules/windows/registry/registry_set/registry_set_timeproviders_dllname.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, live Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceRegistryEvents
| where (RegistryKey contains "\\Services\\W32Time\\TimeProviders" and RegistryKey endswith "\\DllName") and (not((RegistryValueData in~ ("%SystemRoot%\\System32\\vmictimeprovider.dll", "%systemroot%\\system32\\w32time.dll", "C:\\Windows\\SYSTEM32\\w32time.DLL"))))
